## Proof

The answer is **yes** for every positive integer $n$.

**Key observation.** Every line segment with rational endpoints contains infinitely many rational points. Specifically, if $P, Q \in \mathbb{Q}^2$ with $P \neq Q$, the rational points on segment $[P,Q]$ are exactly $\{(1-t)P + tQ : t \in \mathbb{Q} \cap [0,1]\}$, which is infinite.

**Setup.** Since $\mathbb{Q}^2$ is countable, the set of all line segments with rational endpoints is countable. Enumerate them as $S_1, S_2, S_3, \ldots$

**Construction.** We use colors $\{1, 2, \ldots, n\}$ and build the coloring inductively.

*Initial step.* Color every rational point with color $1$. Let $R = \emptyset$ be a set of **reserved** points (points that will never be recolored).

*Step $k$* ($k = 1, 2, 3, \ldots$): We ensure segment $S_k$ contains all $n$ colors while preserving the property for all previous segments $S_1, \ldots, S_{k-1}$.

**(a)** Pick a point $r_k$ on $S_k$ that currently has color $1$ and is not in $R$. Add $r_k$ to $R$. This point is permanently reserved, guaranteeing $S_k$ always has color $1$.

**(b)** For each color $j \in \{2, \ldots, n\}$ not yet appearing on $S_k$: pick a point $q$ on $S_k$ that currently has color $1$, is not in $R$, and was not already chosen in this step. Recolor $q$ to $j$.

**(c) Feasibility of the choices.** At the start of step $k$:
- $|R| = k - 1$ (one reserved point per previous step).
- At most $(n-1)(k-1)$ points have been recolored in all previous steps (at most $n-1$ per step).

So at most $(n-1)(k-1) + (k-1) = n(k-1)$ points on $S_k$ are unavailable (already recolored or reserved). Since $S_k$ contains **infinitely** many rational points, there are always enough available color-$1$ points to make all required choices. $\checkmark$

**Verification.** We claim that after all steps complete, every segment $S_k$ contains all $n$ colors.

- **Color $1$:** The reserved point $r_k \in R$ lies on $S_k$ and is never recolored (we never recolor points in $R$). So $S_k$ permanently contains color $1$. $\checkmark$

- **Colors $2, \ldots, n$:** Each color $j \in \{2,\ldots,n\}$ is assigned to some point on $S_k$ at step $k$ (if not already present from an earlier step). Once a point is recolored to $j \neq 1$, it is **never recolored again** — at every subsequent step we only recolor points with color $1$, and this point no longer has color $1$. So $S_k$ permanently contains color $j$. $\checkmark$

- **Preservation for previous segments $S_i$ ($i < k$):** At step $k$, we only recolor points with color $1$ that are not in $R$. This preserves:
  - Color $1$ on $S_i$: via the reserved point $r_i \in R$, which is never touched. $\checkmark$
  - Colors $2, \ldots, n$ on $S_i$: via points recolored at step $i$, which have colors $\neq 1$ and are therefore never recolored again. $\checkmark$

Since every line segment with rational endpoints appears in the enumeration $\{S_k\}$, **every such segment contains rational points of all $n$ colors**.

**The case $n = 1$** is trivial: a single color suffices since every segment contains rational points.

This completes the proof for all positive integers $n$. $\blacksquare$

